======================================================================
KEY FINDINGS:
======================================================================

1. SPONTANEOUS CURVATURE AND PACKING PARAMETER:
   - PC: P = 1.0, C_0 = 0 (cylindrical, bilayer)
   - PE: P = 1.5, C_0 = -0.5 nm^-1 (inverted cone, inverted micelle)
   - PI: P = 0.8, C_0 = 0.3 nm^-1 (cone, micelle)
   - CL: P = 1.8, C_0 = -0.8 nm^-1 (highly inverted, cristae)
   - Packing parameter P = v / (a_0 * l_c)

2. INVERSE MICELLE FORMATION AND TRANSPORTER ASSEMBLY:
   - Optimal PE fraction: ~30-40%
   - Delta G < -5 kT required for transporter assembly
   - PE provides negative curvature for protein insertion
   - Hydrophobic effect favorable, electrostatic unfavorable
   - Curvature matching stabilizes membrane proteins

3. METABOLIC COST VS FUNCTIONAL BENEFIT:
   - PC: 4 ATP, low benefit (structural base)
   - PE: 3.5 ATP, high benefit (transporters)
   - PS: 4.5 ATP, medium benefit (signaling)
   - PI: 5 ATP, high benefit (signaling, curvature)
   - CL: 8 ATP, very high benefit (mitochondrial cristae)
   - Functional benefit = |C_0| + |P - 1|

4. SURFACTANT PHASE DIAGRAM:
   - PC: T_m = 270 K (low melting temp)
   - PE: T_m = 310 K (high melting temp)
   - Physiological: T = 310 K, S ~ 0.2-0.3 (fluid phase)
   - PE increases order, tighter packing
   - Fluid phase required for membrane dynamics

======================================================================
CONCLUSION: Lipid physical chemistry determines membrane
assembly, transporter formation, and phase behavior. Evolution
balances metabolic cost against functional benefit.
